# Workflow: Test, Lint, Commit

## After every meaningful batch of work

Run tests and lint before committing. "Meaningful batch" means any change
that affects runtime behaviour, types, or project structure — not just
formatting or comment edits.

```bash
deno test --allow-all   # all workspace members
deno lint
```

Fix every failure before proceeding. Do not commit broken or lint-failing code.

If tests don't exist yet for the changed code, lint is sufficient — but note
the absence in the commit message.

## Commit only when clean

Only create a commit once tests and lint pass. One logical unit of work per
commit — do not bundle unrelated changes.

## Commit message format

```
<short summary of what changed> (≤72 chars, imperative mood)

<why this change was made, or what problem it solves>
<notable implications, risks, or follow-up work needed>
```

- First line: imperative mood ("Add", "Fix", "Refactor"), ≤72 characters.
- Blank line between summary and body.
- Body: 2–4 concise lines. Focus on *why* and *implications*, not *what*
  (the diff already shows what).
- No bullet points in the body unless listing genuinely distinct items.

### Examples

```
Add use case and invoker for content ingestion

Implements the ingest_content use case and Hono HTTP invoker.
Config reads LLM_API_KEY at startup; missing key throws immediately.
No tests yet — queue repository stub needed first.
```

```
Fix feed position monotonicity on snooze

snooze_feed_item RPC was computing max(position) without locking,
causing duplicate positions under concurrent requests.
Changed to FOR UPDATE to serialise the update.
```

## Do not commit

- `.env`, `.env.local`, or any file containing secrets.
- `deno.lock` conflicts left unresolved.
- Files unrelated to the current unit of work.
